<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.2: Modeling with Equations to Find Edges in Platonic Solids</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00071</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.2: Modeling with Equations to Find Edges in Platonic Solids</md:title>
    <md:uuid>86afa7cf-62c9-4bae-9a87-7ec8fba8a7aa</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
    <title>Activity</title>
      <para>These three figures are called Platonic solids.</para>

<table>
<tgroup cols="3">
<tbody>
<row>
<entry>Tetrahedron</entry>
<entry>Cube</entry>
<entry>Dodecahedron</entry>
</row>
<row>
<entry><media alt="A tetrahedron.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_04_03_01.svg" />
            </media></entry>
<entry><media alt="A cube.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_04_03_02.svg" />
            </media></entry>
<entry><media alt="A dodecahderon. A 3-D shape with 12 pentagonal faces.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_04_03_03.svg" />
            </media></entry>
</row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
      </table>
      
      <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis />
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>faces</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>vertices</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>edges</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>tetrahedron</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry>6</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>cube</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry />
            <entry />
            <entry />
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>dodecahedron</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>12</entry>
            <entry>20</entry>
            <entry>30</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <para>The table shows the number of faces, vertices, and edges for the tetrahedron and
        dodecahedron.</para>
      <para>Determine the following values regarding the cube.</para>
    
        
        <exercise id="a4de0230-e91f-48b4-bbfc-e1250afb4a9b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Number of faces. </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 6.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: 6</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="cdf77a10-ae30-4d5e-b741-0050e643db28" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Number of edges. </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 8.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: 8</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_13797257-27a3-4f32-adc3-da4166352174" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Number of vertices. </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 12.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: 12</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <para>There are some interesting relationships between the number of faces (\( F \)), edges
        (\( E \)), and vertices (\( V \)) in all Platonic solids. </para>
      
      <!-- Q4 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
        <para>What do you notice about the relationships between the numbers of faces, edges, and
          vertices of the Platonic solids?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para> Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
          <list>
            <item>I noticed all the numbers are even.</item>
            <item>I noticed the number of faces is less than or equal to the number of vertices, or
              \(F \leq V\)</item>
            <item>I noticed the number of edges is always greater than the number of faces, or \(E
              \gt F\).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
<!-- Q5 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
        <para>What do you wonder about the relationships between the numbers of faces, edges, and
          vertices of the Platonic solids?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para> Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
          <list>
            <item>I wonder if the number of edges is always less than the sum of the number of faces
              and the number of vertices, or \(E \lt F + V\). </item>
            <item>I wonder if there is an equation that connects all of the variables.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Q6 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> There is a relationship that can be expressed with an equation using all three
            values: \(F, V, E\). Write the equation that represents how the three values are
            related.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The number of edges is 2 less than the sum of the other two numbers, or \(V + F - 2
            = E\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
    <section>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <para>There are two more Platonic solids: an octahedron, which has 8 faces that are all triangles, and an icosahedron, which has 20 faces that are all triangles.</para>
        <exercise id="e9f36468-a618-4a55-b863-9bf452b3b22c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many edges would each of these solids have? (Keep in mind that each edge is
              used in two faces.)</para>
            <para>The octahedron has how many edges? </para>
            <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The octahedron has 12 edges.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: The octahedron has 12 edges.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="ea62c583-6289-4dca-904c-a02e8d2aa5a4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The icosahedron has how many edges? </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The icosahedron has 30 edges.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: The icosahedron has 30 edges.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="b87cc191-53ed-4e73-b056-a9786c8409a7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use your discoveries from the activity to determine how many vertices each of
              these solids would have.</para>
            <para>The octahedron has how many vertices? </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The octahedron has 6 vertices.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: The octahedron has 6 vertices.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_7a2261b6-0c6f-4594-99b2-7534849eed92" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The icosahedron has how many vertices? </para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The icosahedron has 30 vertices.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: The icosahedron has 30 vertices.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section>
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>For all 5 Platonic solids, determine how many faces meet at each vertex.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The tetrahedron, cube, and dodecahedron have 3 faces meeting at each vertex. The
            octahedron has 4 faces meeting at each vertex. The icosahedron has 5 faces meeting at
            each vertex.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
